// The `prelude.crypto` sub-module: the cryptographic primitives no Katari composition can express.
// Policy — what to hash, whether to key it — stays in the program.

@"The SHA-256 digest of @text@'s UTF-8 bytes, as 64 lowercase hex characters. Deterministic and pure,
so a replay recomputes the same digest. @text@ is public by type. A digest of an enumerable input (a
user id, an email) is reversible by dictionary; `hmac_sha256` is the irreversible form."
primitive agent sha256(@"The text to digest, hashed as its UTF-8 bytes." text: string) -> string

@"The HMAC-SHA-256 tag of @text@ under the secret @key@, as 64 lowercase hex characters —
irreversible even over an enumerable input, unlike `sha256`. Deterministic, so a replay recomputes the
same tag. The result is public despite the private key: irreversibility is the sound declassification,
so this is the one prim whose result the runtime does not taint private."
primitive agent hmac_sha256(
  @"The secret key material; the tag is stable per key and unforgeable without it." key: string of private,
  @"The text to tag, as its UTF-8 bytes; public, like `sha256`'s." text: string,
) -> string

@"A short, stable, irreversible stand-in for @value@ under the secret @key@ — the first @length@ hex
characters of its HMAC-SHA-256 tag. Keyed rather than a plain `sha256` because the thing being
pseudonymised is an identifier, which is enumerable. @length@ is a collision / brevity trade, not a
security one (8 hex characters distinguish tens of thousands of subjects, so widen it for a bigger
population); rotating @key@ rotates every pseudonym, which is the erasure story and the reason the key
belongs in the project's secret env rather than in the source. The result is public, like
`hmac_sha256`'s."
agent pseudonym(
  @"The secret key the pseudonym is stable under; rotating it rotates every pseudonym." key: string of private,
  @"The identifier to stand in for — a user id, an address; public, and enumerable." value: string,
  @"How many hex characters to keep; a collision / brevity trade, not a security one." length: integer ?= 8,
) -> string {
  string.slice(value = hmac_sha256(key = key, text = value), start = 0, end = length)
}
